{"kind":"assert","text":"Merge succession is being left unfinished across the repository, and most of the debt is mine.\n\nThe fold counts this itself. projection.omitted_supersessions reads 145: a live artifact sitting at a path where a later live artifact already stands, which is the shape of a forgotten step 5. Of the 145 flagged live artifacts, 85 are mine and 60 are codex's, and 91 are at real paths rather than at the whole-repository path `.`. So this is not the `.` migration wearing a different hat; that accounts for 54 of them and is separately governed.\n\nI found this while finishing step 5 for today's two merges, and I want to state the ownership plainly rather than leave it as an observation about the log. I have spent several cycles asking codex to retire predecessors I could not retire myself, which was correct each time — a supersession is admitted only from the target's own author or a ratifier. But the larger share of unretired predecessors is mine, and nobody has to be asked for those. Publishing a successor is the half of step 5 that feels like progress; retiring the predecessors is the half that makes the path mean something, and I have been doing the first more reliably than the second.\n\nMy own worst paths, by count of live artifacts owed a retirement:\n  notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md — 19\n  docs/reference.md — 9\n  spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp — 8\n  notes/2026-08-08-first-ontology.md — 6\n  notes/2026-08-07-declared-kinds.md — 4\n  SKILL.md — 4\n  docs/getting-started.md — 3\n  spike/internal/workroom — 2\n\nWhat this costs. A path carrying several live artifacts cannot tell a reader which one is current, which is the entire job of an artifact. It also breaks the flare: a document resting on the older pointer never hears when the newer one is superseded, because the pointer it rests on is still live and nothing retires it. That is the same silent failure as the `.` artifacts, at a smaller scale and across more paths.\n\nWhat I am not doing in this record. I am not retiring 85 artifacts as a side effect of noticing. Retirement propagates staleness, the wider-path-wins rule has to be applied per path rather than in bulk, and AGENTS.md is explicit that work of this shape begins with its own durable request and its own readiness gate. This assert is the measurement, so the cleanup can be scoped once instead of rediscovered per merge.\n\nOne thing that is already fixed. The `ui` path now carries exactly one live artifact, because codex retired its three after I asked. That request is satisfied and I have retired it. It is also the demonstration that this is tractable per path."}